.Dd October 7, 2026
.Dt PMC.LBR 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pmc.lbr
.Nd Last Branch Record capture for AMD CPUs
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libpmc
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In pmc.h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
AMD Last Branch Record version 2 (LBR v2) records taken branches in a
per-CPU ring of From/To address pairs.
Capture is supported only on amd64 kernels, on processors that advertise
PerfMonV2 and LbrExtV2 with an LBR depth from 1 through 16.
The ring freezes when a core counter overflows on processors with
LbrAndPmcFreeze, so the records describe the branches leading up to each
sampling interrupt.
.Pp
LBR capture is not a PMC class of its own.
It is requested with the
.Li lbr
event qualifier, which requests
.Dv PMC_CAP_LBR ,
on a core counter allocated in thread-sampling
.Pq Dv PMC_MODE_TS
or system-sampling
.Pq Dv PMC_MODE_SS
mode.
For example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pmcstat -P ex_ret_brn_tkn,lbr,usr -O sample.out command
pmcstat -R sample.out
.Ed
.Pp
The qualifier takes no value.
For events named by the pmu-events tables, it may be combined with
.Li usr
and
.Li os ;
other qualifiers on these LBR requests are rejected.
Legacy AMD event names retain the qualifiers documented in
.Xr pmc.amd 3 .
.Pp
Allocation fails on unsupported processors or kernels, for counting modes,
and for PMCs other than AMD core counters, including IBS, L3, and Data Fabric
PMCs.
The
.Li usr
and
.Li os
qualifiers also scope the branch records:
.Li usr
alone suppresses branches ending in CPL 0, and
.Li os
alone suppresses branches ending in CPL greater than 0.
Omitting both qualifiers or specifying both selects the same normalized
filter, which records both user and kernel branches.
.Pp
Branch records that may contain kernel addresses disclose kernel layout and
control flow.
Unless
.Va security.bsd.unprivileged_syspmcs
is set, an LBR PMC without the
.Li usr
qualifier alone requires the same privilege as a system-wide PMC
.Pq Dv PRIV_PMC_SYSTEM ,
even in thread-sampling mode, and is never available inside a jail.
Allocation fails with
.Er EPERM
otherwise.
User-only LBR PMCs follow the ordinary process-mode rules.
.Pp
The LBR_SELECT register is shared by all counters on a CPU, and a
thread-sampling PMC can run on any CPU.
Consequently all allocated thread-sampling LBR PMCs must use the same
normalized filter.
All allocated system-sampling LBR PMCs on a CPU must also use the same filter.
If any thread-sampling LBR PMC is allocated, every system-sampling LBR PMC
on every CPU must match its filter.
System-sampling LBR PMCs on disjoint CPUs may use different filters when no
thread-sampling LBR PMC is allocated.
Filters are reserved at allocation and held until release, including while
the PMC is stopped.
.Pp
Samples taken with LBR active set the
.Dv PMC_CC_F_MULTIPART
flag and embed a payload of type
.Dv PMC_CC_MULTIPART_LBR
in the callchain record, using the layout
described in
.Xr pmc.ibs 3 .
The eight-byte multipart header gives payload lengths in native pointer-sized
slots; each LBR record contains two 64-bit words.
The payload holds up to 16 valid branch records, most recent branch first.
Each record contains the raw From then To register image.
The instruction pointer is bits [57:0] of each word, sign-extended from bit 57.
The kernel rewrites these bits from the processor's implemented linear-address
width, so the result is a canonical address on every processor.
Bit 63 of the From word flags a mispredicted branch; bit 63 of the To word
flags a valid (retired) record and bit 62 a speculative one.
Invalid records are dropped in the kernel and do not appear in the payload.
.Pp
Every sample of an LBR PMC carries an LBR payload part, but the payload
is empty when the ring could not be read for that interrupt: shortly after
a context switch or exec reset, while hwpmc updates the recording state, or
for an overflow of a PMC that was not yet active on the CPU.
On processors with LbrAndPmcFreeze, a delayed interrupt whose frozen ring
was invalidated by a reset also carries an empty payload.
The
.Va kern.hwpmc.amd_lbr_empty_samples
sysctl counts samples that were logged empty for these reasons; an
empty payload after a reset with no branches since is not counted.
The
.Va kern.hwpmc.amd_lbr_depth
sysctl reports the ring depth, or 0 when LBR capture is unavailable.
.Pp
The textual log decoder in
.Xr pmcstat 8
prints an
.Li lbr
entry for each From/To pair and marks mispredicted and speculative records.
Callchain analysis and JSON callchain output use the sampled instruction
pointers following the payload, without treating branch records as stack frames.
Disabling callchain capture with
.Fl N
in
.Xr pmcstat 8
retains the LBR payload and the sampled instruction pointer.
.Pp
A self-attached thread-sampling LBR PMC requires a configured log file before it
can be started.
Applications must call
.Xr pmc_configure_logfile 3
before
.Xr pmc_start 3 .
These samples are written to the PMC log rather than the
.Xr profil 2
buffer, so that the branch payload is retained.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pmc 3 ,
.Xr pmc.amd 3 ,
.Xr pmc.ibs 3 ,
.Xr pmc_configure_logfile 3 ,
.Xr pmc_start 3 ,
.Xr pmclog 3 ,
.Xr hwpmc 4 ,
.Xr pmcstat 8
.Pp
The record format is described in the
.Rs
.%B "Processor Programming Reference (PPR) for AMD Family 19h Model 11h"
.%N "Publication No. 55901"
.%Q "Advanced Micro Devices, Inc."
.Re
.Sh HISTORY
LBR v2 capture support was first introduced in
.Fx 16.0 .
.Sh CAVEATS
LBR records are not tagged with an address-space identifier.
When any LBR PMC is active, hwpmc resets the ring across context switches and
exec to avoid attributing stale branch addresses to the new address space.
On CPUs without LbrAndPmcFreeze the ring is stopped while the interrupt handler
reads it, but records may still include branches taken during interrupt entry.
.Pp
Instruction fusion can make the hardware report a From address that points
several bytes before the actual branch, for example to the comparison in a
fused compare-and-branch pair.
Records are exported without adjusting these addresses.
.Pp
User-only sampling drops records whose From or To address is a kernel
address, in addition to using the hardware CPL filter, which does not
suppress returns from the kernel to user mode.
Native i386 kernels do not support AMD LBR allocation.
.Pp
LBR capture is disabled, and the rest of hwpmc remains usable, when the LBR
registers cannot be read or are already enabled when the module loads,
for example by firmware or a hypervisor.
.Pp
Branches taken by a
.Xr bhyve 8
guest are recorded in the host ring unless the hypervisor virtualizes LBR,
and are attributed to the thread running the virtual CPU.
